.TH "HIGHLIGHTREGISTRY.HIGHLIGHTSFROMPOINT" "3JS" "April 17, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
HighlightRegistry.highlightsFromPoint \- HighlightRegistry:
highlightsFromPoint() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]highlightsFromPoint()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]HighlightRegistry\f[R] interface returns an array of objects
representing the custom highlights applied at a specific point within
the viewport.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
highlightsFromPoint(x, y)
highlightsFromPoint(x, y, options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]x\f[R]
The x\-coordinate of the point within the viewport from which to return
custom highlight information.
.TP
\f[B]y\f[R]
The y\-coordinate of the point within the viewport from which to return
custom highlight information.
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An object containing options, which can include:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]shadowRoots\f[R]
An array of \f[CR]ShadowRoot\f[R] objects.
Custom highlights that exist at the specified point inside shadow roots
in the array will also be included in the return value, in addition to
those present in the light DOM.
By default, highlights inside shadow roots are not returned.
.RE
.SS Return value
An array of \f[CR]HighlightHitResult\f[R] objects representing the
custom highlights applied at the point in the viewport specified by the
\f[CR]x\f[R] and \f[CR]y\f[R] parameters.
.PP
Each \f[CR]HighlightHitResult\f[R] object contains the following
properties:
.TP
\f[B]highlight\f[R]
A \f[CR]Highlight\f[R] object representing the applied custom highlight.
.TP
\f[B]ranges\f[R]
An array of \f[CR]AbstractRange\f[R] objects representing the ranges to
which the custom highlight is applied.
.PP
If no custom highlights are applied at the specified point, or the
specified point is outside the viewport, the method returns an empty
array.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Output custom highlights applied at the mouse pointer position
In this example, you can apply custom highlights to a paragraph of text.
These custom highlights can be overlapping.
When the user double\-clicks the paragraph, we use the
\f[CR]highlightsFromPoint()\f[R] method to return the content of any
custom highlights located at the mouse pointer coordinates of the
double\-click.
.SS HTML
The markup includes a \f[CR]<p>\f[R] element containing text to which
you can apply custom highlights, and a \f[CR]<section>\f[R] element into
which we will output the highlighted text fragments.
.IP
.EX
<h1>highlightsFromPoint() demo</h1>
<p class=\(dqhighlightable\-text\(dq>
  When you select a section of text then press \(dqh\(dq on the keyboard, the text you
  selected will be given a custom highlight. Multiple highlights will be colored
  yellow, red, and blue, in that order. When you double\-click on a highlighted
  section of text, that section will be outputted at the bottom of the UI. If
  multiple highlights overlap the section, you\(aqll see multiple text sections
  outputted.
</p>
<h2>Highlighted text at point</h2>
<section></section>
.EE
.SS CSS
In the CSS, we define styling for three custom highlights named
\f[CR]highlight1\f[R], \f[CR]highlight2\f[R], and \f[CR]highlight3\f[R].
We select each custom highlight by passing its name into the
\f[CR]::highlight()\f[R] pseudo\-element, giving them yellow, red, and
blue background colors respectively.
.IP
.EX
:root::highlight(highlight1) {
  background\-color: rgb(255 255 0 / 0.5);
}

:root::highlight(highlight2) {
  background\-color: rgb(255 0 0 / 0.5);
}

:root::highlight(highlight3) {
  background\-color: rgb(0 0 255 / 0.75);
  color: white;
}
.EE
.SS JavaScript
The script for this example has two distinct areas of functionality: We
first need to create custom highlights and apply them to our content,
then we can use the \f[CR]highlightsFromPoint()\f[R] method to return
custom highlights from a specific point.
.SS Creating and applying custom highlights
To create custom highlights, we start by grabbing references to the
\f[CR]<p>\f[R] element and its contained text node.
We then create a variable called \f[CR]highlightCount\f[R], initially
set to \f[CR]1\f[R], which will be used to specify which custom
highlight to apply later on.
.IP
.EX
const pElem = document.querySelector(\(dq.highlightable\-text\(dq);
const textNode = pElem.firstChild;
let highlightCount = 1;
.EE
.PP
Next, we define a \f[CR]keydown\f[R] event handler that applies a custom
highlight to any selected text if h is pressed on the keyboard.
Inside, we start by grabbing the selected text using
\f[CR]Window.getSelection()\f[R] and converting it to a \f[CR]Range\f[R]
using \f[CR]Selection.getRangeAt()\f[R].
.PP
We check that the \f[CR]selectedRange\f[R] object\(cqs
\f[CR]startContainer\f[R] and \f[CR]endContainer\f[R] are both equal to
the paragraph \f[CR]textNode\f[R], to make sure we don\(cqt allow any
cross\-container highlights.
If so, we set the custom \f[CR]highlightName\f[R] we want to apply to
the \f[CR]selectedRange\f[R] using
\f[CR]highlight${highlightCount++}\f[R].
Since we are incrementing \f[CR]highlightCount\f[R], we add in a check
\(em when it reaches \f[CR]4\f[R], we set it back to \f[CR]1\f[R].
This has the effect of cycling through the available highlights in order
as they are set.
.PP
Finally for the \f[CR]keydown\f[R] event handler, we create a new
\f[CR]highlight\f[R] object using the \f[CR]Highlight()\f[R]
constructor, passing it the \f[CR]selectedRange\f[R] we created earlier.
We then apply the chosen custom highlight referenced in
\f[CR]highlightName\f[R] to \f[CR]highlight\f[R] using the
\f[CR]HighlightRegistry.set()\f[R] method.
.IP
.EX
window.addEventListener(\(dqkeydown\(dq, (event) => {
  if (event.key === \(dqh\(dq) {
    const selection = window.getSelection();
    const selectedRange = selection.getRangeAt(0);
    if (
      selectedRange.startContainer === textNode &&
      selectedRange.endContainer === textNode
    ) {
      const highlightName = \(gahighlight${highlightCount++}\(ga;
      if (highlightCount === 4) {
        highlightCount = 1;
      }
      const highlight = new Highlight(selectedRange);
      CSS.highlights.set(highlightName, highlight);
    }
  }
});
.EE
.SS Returning custom highlights from a point
Now that we have the ability to create and apply custom highlights, we
can use the \f[CR]highlightsFromPoint()\f[R] method to return the custom
highlights applied at a specific point.
.PP
We grab a reference to our \f[CR]<section>\f[R] element, then define a
\f[CR]dblclick\f[R] event handler function to handle outputting the
highlighted text at the mouse cursor position when the event fires.
Inside the handler we pass the current mouse coordinates into a
\f[CR]highlightsFromPoint()\f[R] call, clear the contents of the
\f[CR]<section>\f[R] element, then loop through each highlight in the
\f[CR]highlights\f[R] array.
.PP
For each \f[CR]highlight\f[R], we grab the first range in the
\f[CR]ranges\f[R] array (there is only ever one range in each highlight,
in this case), then get the exact highlighted string using
\f[CR]Range.toString()\f[R] and add it to the \f[CR]<section>\f[R]
element\(cqs \f[CR]innerHTML\f[R], inside an \f[CR]<article>\f[R]
element.
.IP
.EX
const section = document.querySelector(\(dqsection\(dq);

pElem.addEventListener(\(dqdblclick\(dq, (event) => {
  const highlights = CSS.highlights.highlightsFromPoint(
    event.clientX,
    event.clientY,
  );

  section.innerHTML = \(dq\(dq;
  for (highlight of highlights) {
    const range = highlight.ranges[0];
    const textSelection = range.toString();
    section.innerHTML += \(ga<article>${textSelection}</article>\(ga;
  }
});
.EE
